        {
          "text": "The policy separates acceptable hospitality from improper influence primarily by intent and by what is given, not just by dollar value.",
          "support_status": "supported",
          "citation_status": "supported",
          "note": ""
        },
        {
          "text": "The policy states that if the intention is to improperly influence, anything, however small, might be considered 'of value'.",
          "support_status": "supported",
          "citation_status": "supported",
          "note": ""
        },
        {
          "text": "The Code of Ethics prohibits giving 'anything else of value' to anyone 'in order to obtain or keep business, or secure any other improper advantage for GitHub or Microsoft'.",
          "support_status": "supported",
          "citation_status": "supported",
          "note": ""
        },
        {
          "text": "GitHub's business relationships must not be used 'to create a special advantage or influence a deal'.",
          "support_status": "supported",
          "citation_status": "supported",
          "note": ""
        },
        {
          "text": "Examples of acceptable hospitality for customers/prospective customers include swag, economy class airfare to GitHub offices for Executive Briefings, bleacher-type tickets to a sporting event, a reasonably priced dinner in town, and a small box of chocolates as an end-of-year thank you.",
          "support_status": "supported",
          "citation_status": "supported",
          "note": ""
        },
        {
          "text": "Examples of unacceptable gifts include first class airfare, winery tours, tickets to Disneyland/the World Cup/the Olympics, an autographed futbol, and charity contributions.",
          "support_status": "supported",
          "citation_status": "supported",
          "note": ""
        },
        {
          "text": "A reasonably priced dinner in town is listed as acceptable, and bleacher-type tickets to a sporting event are acceptable.",
          "support_status": "supported",
          "citation_status": "supported",
          "note": ""
        },
        {
          "text": "Premium event tickets (e.g., World Cup, Olympics) are listed as not ok.",
          "support_status": "supported",
          "citation_status": "supported",
          "note": ""
        },
        {
          "text": "A sports ticket or dinner could fall on either side depending on its nature and, critically, the intent behind offering it.",
          "support_status": "supported",
          "citation_status": "supported",
          "note": ""
        },
        {
          "text": "No gifts are acceptable for government officials (perhaps stickers), and employees should ask what their policies permit; many U.S. congresspeople will not allow you to take them out for dinner and pay.",
          "support_status": "supported",
          "citation_status": "supported",
          "note": ""
        },
        {
          "text": "The Anti-Bribery Statement references that the Gifts and Entertainment Policy includes clear monetary thresholds for gifts along with annual limitations, with limited exceptions for gifts approved by appropriate management.",
          "support_status": "supported",
          "citation_status": "supported",
          "note": ""
        },
        {
          "text": "The specific threshold amounts are not provided in the supplied evidence.",
          "support_status": "supported",
          "citation_status": "supported",
          "note": ""
        },
        {
          "text": "When in doubt, the policy advises running it by Legal.",
          "support_status": "supported",
          "citation_status": "supported",
          "note": ""
        }
      ]
    }
